elings, and you don't want to give them a reason to be mad at you. You want to make sure that you've severed all ties with the company.\n\n\u2022 If you have a contract, you can always just tell them you are leaving and give them the date you are leaving. \u2013\u00a0Eric Jul 16 '13 at 22:14\n\nI suggest you find out the exact wording of your contract. If it has a \"no surprises\" clause, you could just give them the date you're leaving and let them take it from there.\n\nIf not, you may want to ask them to give you some time to discuss it with your boss. They may not have a problem with it, and you may be able to work something out.\n\n\u2022 I think that if you have a \"no surprises\" clause in your contract, you can just tell the company that you're leaving, and they can take it from there. \u2013\u00a0Mark C Jul 16 '13 at 22:32\n\u2022 I agree with @MarkC. If you have a contract, the company probably has a problem with that. If you don't have a contract, though, you might be able to use \"no surprises\" to your advantage. \u2013\u00a0rmeador Jul 16 '13 at 22:34\n\u2022 @MarkC If you have a contract, you can always just tell them you are leaving and give them the date you are leaving. \u2013\u00a0Eric Jul 16 '13 at 22:35\n\nI'm not sure I'd suggest this, but I'd ask your boss and HR to give you an appropriate amount of time to tell people. This is not a formal termination, just a \"I'm leaving\" email. They'll probably feel better about it than a \"I quit\" letter. It's up to them whether they want to send it to the company or not.\n\n\u2022 You should probably also note that you're leaving before the contract ends. Otherwise, the company could possibly sue you for wrongful termination. \u2013\u00a0Blagovest Buyukliev Jul 16 '13 at 22:39\n\nI think that if you have a contract, you can just tell them you're leaving, and they can take it from there. If you don't have a contract, you might be able to use \"no surprises\" to your advantage.",
